    "summary": "High‑energy '73 Denver show with epic Playin’ sandwich and soaring solos",
    "blurb": "The set delivers a monster Playing in the Band sandwich that weaves Half‑Step, El Paso, Wharf Rat and Morning Dew into one seamless suite. Highlights include Here Comes Sunshine, Weather Report Suite and a scorching Truckin’ > Nobody’s Fault But Mine sequence. Jerry’s lead work is on fire while Keith adds jazzy texture throughout.",
    "review": "The Denver Coliseum show of November 21, 1973 finds the Dead in top form, riding a high‑octane wave from the opening numbers through an unforgettable second set. The first half of the evening builds momentum with tight renditions of Jack Straw, Looks Like Rain and Here Comes Sunshine, while the Weather Report Suite glides smoothly into the set’s centerpiece – a massive Playing in the Band sandwich that stitches Mississippi Half‑Step, El Paso, Wharf Rat and Morning Dew together with seamless segues and expansive improvisation. Jerry Garcia’s guitar soars on every turn, delivering a “melting‑face‑off” solo that rides the crest of the jam, and his lead on Jack Straw and Big River is equally dazzling. Keith Godchaux provides rich, jazzy textures that lift the extended jams, especially during the Playing in the Band/Wharf Rat sequence, while Phil Lesh’s deep, melodic bass underpins the whole performance despite a slight mix dip. Bill Kreutzmann’s drumming stays tight and propulsive, giving the band a solid foundation for the high‑energy explorations. The second set caps off with a blistering Truckin’ → Nobody’s Fault But Mine transition and a rousing One More Saturday Night, leaving the audience buzzing long after the final encore of Uncle John’s Band.",
